Use `$md` when a template body should be formatted as Markdown.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    content = [$md:
        # Welcome

        This text becomes **formatted content**.
    ]
]

Small inline code spans use one backtick on each side.

Use `$code "moth"` or another language name for a full highlighted code block.

Moth Markdown stays small. It does not support fenced code
blocks or pipe tables.

### Section IDs @#section-ids

Add a bare `@#id` at block start or after whitespace to set the HTML `id` of the heading, paragraph or list item that contains it. The marker is zero-width. An ID starts with a Unicode letter and continues with Unicode letters, digits, `_` or `-`, and its authored case is preserved.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    content = [$md:
        ## Getting started @#getting-started

        Jump back to @#getting-started (Getting started).
    ]
]

A paragraph containing only a marker renders a standalone empty `span`.

Unmarked headings have no `id`. `$md` does not check page-wide uniqueness, add generic HTML attributes or validate fragment targets. Plain `.md` does not use section markers.

Advanced covers the full rules.
